
  [1mHow long should the identity live, and what clears it?[0m

  - [1mPer-tab ([38;5;153msessionStorage[39m)[0m — dies when the tab closes. Smallest exposure window; a shared or public machine doesn't
    leak the last user to the next one. Cost: open a second tab and the app doesn't know who you are, which tends to
    surprise people.
  - [1mAcross sessions ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — survives tab close and browser restart, shared across tabs on the origin. This is
    what "persists" usually means and what other forms will expect. Cost: it sits on disk until something explicitly
    clears it, it's readable by any script on the origin (so any XSS reads it), and on a shared machine the next person
    inherits the last user's attribution until they log in.
  - [1mCookie[0m — mainly worth it if the future backend wants the ID sent automatically on every request. For
    attribution-only client-side reads it's the fiddliest of the three, and a JS-set cookie can't be [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m, so it
    buys no XSS protection over [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m,[0m given "persists" and "other forms will need it later" — but paired with two things in
  the design: an explicit clear path (a [38;5;153mlogout()[39m that wipes it, so there's a way to stop being the last user), and a
  rule that nothing ever treats the stored value as proof of anything. Since this is attribution-only, the realistic
  worst case is mislabeled analytics rather than a breach — but the shared-machine case is real, so I want the clear
  path in from the start rather than bolted on.

  Switching between these later is cheap (one storage adapter, if I build it that way). The clear path and the
  never-trust rule are the parts that get expensive to retrofit.
